## Dimension 2: Information Governance

### Applicable Guardrails
- **IG-001:** Data Minimisation — task notifications contain only the minimum information needed (task ID, document type, due date); full document content not included in notifications
- **IG-003:** Lawful Basis Declaration — Article 6(1)(e) public task + Article 9(2)(h) health data
- **IG-004:** Special Category Data Protection — task free-text instructions may reference patient clinical information; stored encrypted at rest

### Data Handling
- Task free-text instructions stored encrypted at rest (may contain clinical context)
- Task notifications contain task metadata only; not full document content or patient clinical data
- Routing rule configuration (conditions + assignee) stored per practice; not shared with or visible to other practices
- Rule sharing between practices is explicitly not supported by the product
- Task trail retained per document retention schedule (NHS IF3005)

### GDPR Articles
- **Article 6(1)(e):** Lawful basis — public task
- **Article 9(2)(h):** Special category health data — medical purposes
- **Article 5(1)(f):** Integrity and confidentiality — task content and rule configuration protected at rest and in transit

---

## Dimension 3: Security

### Applicable Guardrails
- **AUTH-004:** Authorization Required — task creation, rule management, and disclaimer acknowledgement endpoints require valid JWT with correct scope
- **SEC-001:** TLS Encryption — task content, rule configuration, and notifications over TLS 1.2+

### Security Requirements
- Task assignee validation: assignee must be an active user or role-group within the same practice; cross-practice assignment not possible (server-side enforcement)
- Routing rule management restricted to users with Practice Administrator role or above
- Rule configuration scoped to practice; no API endpoint permits reading or writing another practice's rule configuration
- Task free-text instructions sanitised to prevent XSS when rendered in task views
- Rule condition values (e.g. document title string-match) treated as plain string literals; never executed as code or queries

